Tinea Pedis / Athletes Foot (acute - vesiculobullous)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Interdigital maceration/fissuring, Pruritic scaling, Moccasin distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-045
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe blistering and agonizing burning sensation on the soles of the feet, severely limiting ambulation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an acute, rapidly progressive eruption of intensely painful blisters across the soles of the feet, causing severe burning and an inability to walk. The patient feared a severe allergic reaction or chemical burn. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate a severe vesiculobullous tinea pedis flare from acute palmoplantar pustulosis or dyshidrotic eczema, and to provide potent topical therapy to rapidly arrest the blistering and restore the patient's mobility prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The bottoms of my feet are covered in hundreds of tiny, hard blisters. They itch and burn so intensely I can't put any pressure on them at all."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden, highly inflammatory eruption on the plantar surfaces of both feet beginning 48 hours ago. They complain of intensely pruritic erythematous scaling between the toes, but the primary acute issue is the development of widespread vesiculobullous lesions on the instep and plantar vaults. The blisters are tense, deep-seated, and contain clear fluid. The intense inflammation is causing severe burning pain upon weight-bearing. The patient notes an exacerbation following several days of wearing occlusive, unventilated footwear during a period of excessive sweating. There is associated malodour.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired; crawling or using furniture to avoid standing on the soles of the feet.
Social/Safety Concerns: Acute immobility due to severe mechanical pain and burning.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, lying supine with feet elevated, highly anxious regarding the blistering.
Physical Exam: The bilateral plantar surfaces, specifically the insteps and medial vaults, exhibit dozens of tense, deep-seated, 2-5 mm vesiculobullous lesions on an intensely erythematous base. The blisters contain clear, non-purulent fluid. The lateral web spaces (4th and 5th) display prominent interdigital maceration, fissuring, and peeling. There is diffuse fine scaling and hyperkeratosis along the lateral aspects of the feet (moccasin distribution). No spreading cellulitic erythema, ascending lymphangitis, or purulent crusting is present. The toenails demonstrate mild distal subungual hyperkeratosis and yellow discolouration.
Environmental Observations: Thick wool socks and heavy boots visible near the entrance.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Inflammatory Vesiculobullous Tinea Pedis - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Dyshidrotic Eczema (Pompholyx) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent concurrent interdigital maceration, the classic moccasin scaling, and nail involvement, which strongly confirm a primary dermatophyte infection triggering the severe inflammatory vesicular response, rather than a primary endogenous eczema.

Palmoplantar Pustulosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The vesicles contain clear serous fluid rather than primary sterile yellow pustules, and there is clear evidence of classic tinea in the web spaces.

Bullous Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The deep-seated, tense nature of the vesicles (impetigo bullae are flaccid and rupture easily) and the primary intense pruritus/burning rather than a rapidly spreading honey-crusted infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly symptomatic vesiculobullous variant of Tinea Pedis, triggered by occlusion and excessive sweating. Triage accurately recognized the rapid blistering and acute immobility as requiring urgent medical intervention. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment was vital to definitively diagnose the severe inflammatory fungal etiology, ruling out more complex autoimmune blistering disorders or acute severe eczema. By providing immediate clinical reassurance and prescribing a combination of a potent topical steroid (to rapidly shut down the agonizing vesicular inflammation) and an antifungal, the intervention safely restored the patient's mobility and prevented an inevitable, highly painful emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a potent topical corticosteroid (Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment) to be applied strictly to the blistering areas BD for exactly 5 days to rapidly suppress the severe inflammatory reaction. Prescribed topical Terbinafine 1% cream to be applied to the entire foot (soles and web spaces) daily for 4 weeks.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying this strong steroid ointment exactly twice a day for only 5 days, you will forcefully turn off the massive allergic reaction causing the blisters, instantly stopping the burning pain. By wearing open-toed shoes or walking barefoot at home for the next week, you will completely remove the hot, sweaty environment the fungus relies on, allowing the blisters to dry up and heal rapidly.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to assess the resolution of the blisters and ensure the ongoing antifungal treatment is effective.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the clear fluid in the blisters turns into thick yellow pus, if the red areas become deeply hot and rapidly spread up the ankle, or if you develop a high fever, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe blistering is an extreme immune reaction to the fungus. The blisters will dry out and peel extensively over the next two weeks.
Clinical Course: The intense burning pain will subside within 48 hours of starting the steroid ointment. The underlying fungal infection will require a full month of the antifungal cream to be completely eradicated.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not intentionally pop or drain the blisters, as the intact roof protects the raw skin underneath from serious bacterial infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The heavy sweating in your boots caused the normal athlete's foot fungus to trigger a massive, explosive allergic reaction in your skin, resulting in these deep blisters. The short course of strong steroid ointment will act like a fire extinguisher to stop the blistering, while the antifungal cream will kill the root cause.
Actionable Steps: Keep your feet entirely exposed to the air whenever possible. Wash all your socks in hot water and spray your boots with an antifungal spray.

